From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: bringup <bringup@t1100.local>
Date: Fri, 30 Oct 2026 18:59:43 +0300
Subject: [PATCH] regulator: mt6358: keep registering the remaining rails after
 an error

mt6358_regulator_probe() returns on the first devm_regulator_register()
failure, so a single unusable rail removes *every* MT6358 regulator from the
system: consumers then get -EPROBE_DEFER forever and eMMC, USB, the display
and the GPU never probe. That is exactly what happened with the
regulator-coupled-with experiment (generic_coupler_attach() returns -ENOTSUPP
for a non always-on rail, VGPU is number six) - one property cost the whole
PMIC, and the boot log only had the one "failed to register VGPU" line.

Since a rail that fails to register is a board/PMIC description problem and
not a reason to lose the rails that do work, log the name, count it and
continue. Only report failure if *nothing* registered. A summary line tells
how many rails made it, and dmesg/regulator_summary then name the culprit.

Same treatment for mt6358_sync_vcn33_setting(): VCN33 is the Wi-Fi/BT LDO and
has no business aborting the probe of VCORE/VDRAM/VEMC.

Found on the Fplus T1100 (MT6771 + MT6358) bring-up, where the boot hangs
while the regulators are registered and the log is the only diagnostic.
---
 drivers/regulator/mt6358-regulator.c | 32 +++++++++++++++++++++++-----
 1 file changed, 27 insertions(+), 5 deletions(-)

diff --git a/drivers/regulator/mt6358-regulator.c b/drivers/regulator/mt6358-regulator.c
index d6a0ec406..aec28e525 100644
--- a/drivers/regulator/mt6358-regulator.c
+++ b/drivers/regulator/mt6358-regulator.c
@@ -686,7 +686,7 @@ static int mt6358_regulator_probe(struct platform_device *pdev)
 	struct regulator_config config = {};
 	struct regulator_dev *rdev;
 	const struct mt6358_regulator_info *mt6358_info;
-	int i, max_regulator, ret;
+	int i, max_regulator, ret, registered = 0, failed = 0;
 
 	switch (mt6397->chip_id) {
 	case MT6358_CHIP_ID:
@@ -702,9 +702,14 @@ static int mt6358_regulator_probe(struct platform_device *pdev)
 		return -EINVAL;
 	}
 
+	/*
+	 * VCN33 is the Wi-Fi/BT LDO and has nothing to do with the rails the
+	 * system boots from: a failure here must not cost us the whole PMIC.
+	 */
 	ret = mt6358_sync_vcn33_setting(&pdev->dev);
 	if (ret)
-		return ret;
+		dev_warn(&pdev->dev,
+			 "failed to sync VCN33 setting: %d, continuing\n", ret);
 
 	for (i = 0; i < max_regulator; i++) {
 		config.dev = &pdev->dev;
@@ -714,12 +719,29 @@ static int mt6358_regulator_probe(struct platform_device *pdev)
 					       &mt6358_info[i].desc,
 					       &config);
 		if (IS_ERR(rdev)) {
-			dev_err(&pdev->dev, "failed to register %s\n",
-				mt6358_info[i].desc.name);
-			return PTR_ERR(rdev);
+			/*
+			 * One unusable rail must not take the other ~40 with it:
+			 * everything downstream (eMMC, USB, display, GPU) gets
+			 * -EPROBE_DEFER forever if the provider never shows up.
+			 * Name the culprit and keep going; regulator_summary and
+			 * the log then say which rail is broken.
+			 */
+			dev_err(&pdev->dev, "failed to register %s: %ld, continuing\n",
+				mt6358_info[i].desc.name, PTR_ERR(rdev));
+			failed++;
+			continue;
 		}
+		registered++;
 	}
 
+	if (!registered) {
+		dev_err(&pdev->dev, "no regulator registered at all\n");
+		return -ENODEV;
+	}
+
+	dev_info(&pdev->dev, "%d of %d regulators registered, %d failed\n",
+		 registered, max_regulator, failed);
+
 	return 0;
 }
 
